Because every request already passes through the edge, the CDN can:
- run a WAF that blocks SQL injection (' OR 1=1 --), XSS and scanners, and virtually patches zero-days like Log4j with a rule before the code is fixed;
- apply rate limiting on login, OTP, registration and orders (sliding window or token bucket rather than fixed window);
- absorb DDoS attacks (e.g. ~31 Tbps) across its distributed network;
- terminate TLS, compress, strip banner headers (X-Powered-By), add geo/ASN headers, enforce CORS;
- act as an API gateway: verify JWTs and cookies at the edge, fail over between origins on high 5xx rate or latency, and run edge compute (Workers, Lambda@Edge).
All of this needs origin lockdown: the origin must only accept traffic from CDN IP ranges, or attackers simply bypass the edge.
Risks: the CDN holds your TLS private keys and can impersonate you; it's a single point of failure (a CDN outage takes your site down); it has takedown power over you; vendor lock-in grows as you depend on its WAF, Workers and rules; and a misconfigured cache key can cause cache poisoning.
